{"parentUuid":"b092bed7-5168-45be-9d0b-1aa4ea930481","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"5e3c73d2-3b6f-4caf-a6f1-6fc6c99fca95","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"twinkling-foraging-moonbeam","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01AFft4gXsfQ8Ez9y85mKSR6","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→By Norman R. Cox M.S.M.E.\n     2→\n     3→\fc\n     4→\n     5→L\n     6→\n     7→\fTABLE OF CONTENTS\n     8→SECTION I .\n     9→TECHNOLOGY\n    10→\n    11→PAGE\n    12→\n    13→How Do You Achieve High Quality Solder Joints? .......................................................................................\n    14→\n    15→2\n    16→\n    17→What is the Importance of the Profile? ...........................................................................................................\n    18→\n    19→3\n    20→\n    21→How Many Heating Zones are Required? ......................................................................................................\n    22→\n    23→4\n    24→\n    25→.\n    26→\n    27→What is the Profile Process Band? .................................................................................................................\n    28→\n    29→5\n    30→\n    31→.\n    32→\n    33→What are the Causes of Non-Uniform Heating? ............................................................................................\n    34→\n    35→6\n    36→\n    37→What are the Modes of Heating? ....................................................................................................................\n    38→\n    39→7\n    40→\n    41→How Does Infrared Heating Work? ................................................................................................................\n    42→\n    43→8\n    44→\n    45→How Does Convection Heating Work? ..........................................................................................................\n    46→\n    47→9\n    48→\n    49→What are the Benefits of IR? ... Convection? ................................................................................................\n    50→\n    51→10\n    52→\n    53→What are the Benefits of a Nitrogen Atmosphere? ........................................................................................\n    54→\n    55→11\n    56→\n    57→.\n    58→\n    59→.\n    60→\n    61→SECTION I1 .\n    62→EQUIPMENT SELECTION\n    63→What are the Steps in the Selection Process? ................................................................................................\n    64→\n    65→12\n    66→\n    67→How Can You Evaluate Costs? .....................................................................................................................\n    68→\n    69→13\n    70→\n    71→Selection Criteria Chart .................................................................................................................................\n    72→\n    73→14\n    74→\n    75→.\n    76→\n    77→SECTION I11 .\n    78→RESEARCH. INC EQUIPMENT TECHNOLOGY\n    79→Convection Oven Thermal Control ...............................................................................................................\n    80→\n    81→15\n    82→\n    83→Convection Oven with Nitrogen Atmosphere ...............................................................................................\n    84→\n    85→16\n    86→\n    87→IR/Convection Oven Closed Loop Control ...................................................................................................\n    88→\n    89→17\n    90→\n    91→IR/Convection Oven with Nitrogen Atmosphere ..........................................................................................\n    92→\n    93→18\n    94→\n    95→References .....................................................................................................................................................\n    96→\n    97→19\n    98→\n    99→Edge Conveyor ..............................................................................................................................................\n   100→\n   101→19\n   102→\n   103→;............................\n   104→\n   105→20\n   106→\n   107→Product Matrix ..................................................................................................................\n   108→\n   109→Printed in the U.S.A. 0 Research. Inc., 1992\n   110→\n   111→1\n   112→\n   113→\fIN\n   114→I TECHNOLOGY\n   115→\n   116→~\n   117→\n   118→HOW DO YOU ACHIEVE HIGH QUALITY SOLDER JOINTS?\n   119→The process of producing high quality solder joints is\n   120→similar to the process of learning how to ride a bicycle.\n   121→If you do not understand the fundamentals, it is virtually\n   122→impossible. However, once you understand and implement the basics, the results will follow. The goal of all\n   123→SMT process engineers is to repeatedly produce high\n   124→quality solder joints that have fine grain structure and\n   125→excellent wetting characteristics, as shown in the figure\n   126→below:\n   127→\n   128→SURFACES MUST BE\n   129→CLEAN AND SOLDERABLE\n   130→II\n   131→\n   132→1\n   133→\n   134→MANPOWER\n   135→The manpower must be properly trained. All machines in\n   136→the line require knowledgeable operators and maintenance personnel. A machine is only as good as the person\n   137→operating it.\n   138→\n   139→MUST BE\n   140→SMALL\n   141→\n   142→CONTROL VARIABLES\n   143→In order to produce high quality solderjoints, all process\n   144→variables must be in control. The five general process\n   145→variables (l) that need to be in control include the materials, machines, manpower, methods, and the environment. If the process variables are not in control, there is\n   146→a very good chance that soldering defects will exist.\n   147→Let’s take a closer look at each.\n   148→\n   149→MATERIALS\n   150→The materials must meet functional specifications so\n   151→that the product will perform to specification. Of equal\n   152→importance is the solderability of the assembly. The\n   153→solder paste/flux system must be compatible with the\n   154→base materials of the parts to be soldered. The solder\n   155→paste and parts should be as fresh as possible to enhance\n   156→solderability.\n   157→\n   158→2\n   159→\n   160→MACHINES\n   161→The machines used in the process must be able to perform\n   162→to their specifications. The machines should operate\n   163→within the range for which they are designed. Pushing a\n   164→machine beyond its operating limits will ultimately cause\n   165→a problem. The machines must be reliable and serviceable.\n   166→\n   167→METHODS\n   168→The methods and procedures must be defined and strictly\n   169→adhered to. Inventory procedures should be implemented\n   170→to ensure that components and boards do not stay on the\n   171→shelf too long. Problems should be fully documented\n   172→including all data that pertains to it.\n   173→ENVIRONMENT\n   174→Finally, the processing environment must be controlled.\n   175→This includes both the environment inside and outside of\n   176→the machines. For example, if the humidity in the soldering area is too high, this can cause the solder paste to\n   177→absorbmoisture, whichmay causeproblems duringreflow.\n   178→If the temperature in the room is not controlled, the\n   179→thermal profile in the reflow oven may be affected, which\n   180→again may cause a defect.\n   181→The purpose of this text is to address one aspect of high\n   182→quality SMT soldering, the reflow process.\n   183→\n   184→Producing high quality solder joints\n   185→requires that five general process\n   186→variables need to be in control; the\n   187→materials, machines, manpower,\n   188→methods, and the environment.\n   189→\n   190→Section I - Technology\n   191→\n   192→\fWHAT IS THE IMPORTANCE OF THE PROFILE?\n   193→In order to fully understand Surface Mount Technology\n   194→(SMT) reflow, one needs to have an understanding of\n   195→what is being accomplished. One can take too simplistic\n   196→a viewpoint in that all you need to do is to heat solder\n   197→to a temperature greater than its melting point. One can\n   198→also take too complicated a viewpoint in believing that a\n   199→certain wavelength of infrared (IR) is required, or that a\n   200→certain percentage of convectionis required. Perhaps the\n   201→most astute comment made on surfacemount reflow was\n   202→by an individual whom had just completed an evaluation\n   203→of vapor phase, panel IR, and lamp IR equipment nearly\n   204→five years ago. After coming up with virtually identical\n   205→soldering defect results on all three types of soldering\n   206→equipment, the following comment was made: “If you\n   207→could hit the correct reflow profile using buckets of hot\n   208→water, you would accomplish high quality, low defect\n   209→solder joints.” Although the above comment was made\n   210→in jest (hot water is not quite hot enough to reflow solder),\n   211→there is considerable truth to what was said.\n   212→\n   213→REFLOW PROFILE REQUIREMENT\n   214→High quality, low defect soldering requires identifying\n   215→the optimum temperatureprofile for reflowing the solder\n   216→paste, and repeating over and over. Every solderjoint on\n   217→every board, needs to be heated similarily if the desired\n   218→soldering results are to be accomplished. It does not\n   219→matter how you apply the heat to the solderjoint. What\n   220→does matter is that the heat is applied to the solder joint\n   221→in a controlled manner. The heating and cooling rise\n   222→rates must be compatible with the solder paste and\n   223→components. The amount of time that the assembly is\n   224→exposed to certain temperatures must be defined and\n   225→maintained. In other words, the solder reflow profile\n   226→must first be defined and then maintained.\n   227→\n   228→High quality, low defect soldering\n   229→requires identifying the optimum\n   230→temperature profile for reflowing\n   231→the solder paste, and repeating it\n   232→over and over.\n   233→\n   234→Section I - Technology\n   235→\n   236→280-\n   237→\n   238→‘\n   239→\n   240→240T 220-\n   241→\n   242→I\n   243→\n   244→I\n   245→\n   246→I\n   247→\n   248→I\n   249→\n   250→I\n   251→\n   252→I\n   253→\n   254→1 ;\n   255→\n   256→.’\n   257→\n   258→WETTING ;\n   259→\n   260→I\n   261→\n   262→260-\n   263→\n   264→TIME\n   265→\n   266→:\n   267→\n   268→30-60\n   269→SEC\n   270→\n   271→I\n   272→\n   273→:I\n   274→\n   275→,\n   276→~\n   277→\n   278→I\n   279→\n   280→I\n   281→\n   282→I\n   283→\n   284→I\n   285→\n   286→I\n   287→\n   288→TYPICAL PROFILE\n   289→A typical profile consists of a preheat, dryout, and reflow\n   290→section (zone) where different heating rates are used. In\n   291→the preheat section, the goal is to fully preheat the SMT\n   292→assembly to temperatures generally between 100°C to\n   293→150°C. The most critical parameter in the preheat section\n   294→is to minimize the rate of rise to less than 2”C/second.\n   295→The main concern is minimizing thermal shock on the\n   296→components of the assembly. Multilayer ceramic chip\n   297→capacitors can be vulnerable to cracking if heated too\n   298→fast. Solder paste can spatter also, if heated too fast.\n   299→The second heating section, referred to as the dryout,\n   300→soak, or preflow zone, is used primarily to ensure that\n   301→the solder paste is fully dried before hitting reflow\n   302→temperatures. This zone also acts as a flux activation\n   303→zone for some solder pastes. Solder reflow is accomplished in the reflow zone, where the solder paste is\n   304→elevated to a temperature greater than the melting point\n   305→of the solder. For 63/37 Eutectic solder, the melting\n   306→temperature is 183°C. This temperature must be exceeded by approximately 20°C to ensure quality reflow.\n   307→The amount of time the solder joint is above the melting\n   308→point is referred to as the “wetting time”. The wetting\n   309→time is normally 30 to 60 seconds for most pastes. If the\n   310→wetting time is excessive, intermetallics may occur in\n   311→the joint, which result in brittle solder joints. In summary, define the profile, and hit it.\n   312→\n   313→3\n   314→\n   315→\fHOW MANY HEATING ZONES ARE REQUIRED?\n   316→This question frequently arrives and can be a source of\n   317→confusion. One theory is that if ten zones are good, then\n   318→twenty zones are better. While in theory this may be true,\n   319→in practicality it may not be true. While it improves the\n   320→flexibility of oven, it also adds to the complexity and\n   321→makes oven set-up more difficult.\n   322→Let’s review this question from the standpoint of how\n   323→many zones are really needed. Reflowing solder paste\n   324→typically requires two or three different heating rates to\n   325→accomplish the process. In the figure below, there are\n   326→three “typical” profile bands for RMA, water soluble,\n   327→and no-clean solder pastes. These profiles are rather\n   328→generic, and one should consult with their solder paste\n   329→vendor to determine the desired profile. All of the pastes\n   330→in the figure could be successfully reflowed using\n   331→three zones.\n   332→\n   333→reflow is that if you can control your top and bottom\n   334→heaters separately, then double sided reflow can be\n   335→accomplished (without reflowing side A twice). This\n   336→will not work with most SMT reflow processes, as most\n   337→products are too thermally thin. Independent topbottom\n   338→heating control does help in machines with dual conveyors that are located at different heights in the chamber.\n   339→Product warpage may also be minimized through proper\n   340→control. The bottom line is that most machines have this\n   341→feature, but most often the top and bottom heaters will be\n   342→set at the same value.\n   343→Edge heating zones become significant when edge conveyors are used. Edge conveyors act as heat sinks on the\n   344→edge of the product. Two zones of edge heat (one front,\n   345→one back) will assist in compensating for edge conveyors. If mesh conveyors are used, these zones are\n   346→unnecessary unless the product width is quite wide.\n   347→\n   348→A second issue is the question of independent top and\n   349→bottom heat control. A common conception in SMT\n   350→\n   351→200\n   352→\n   353→c\n   354→I\n   355→I\n   356→I\n   357→I\n   358→I\n   359→I\n   360→I\n   361→I\n   362→I\n   363→I\n   364→I\n   365→\n   366→I\n   367→\n   368→PREHEAT1\n   369→\n   370→I\n   371→\n   372→PREHEAT2\n   373→\n   374→I REFLOW COOLING I\n   375→\n   376→TIME\n   377→\n   378→4\n   379→\n   380→Section I - Technology\n   381→\n   382→\fWHAT IS THE PROFILE PROCESS BAND?\n   383→In reality the profile is not a line, but rather a band or\n   384→process window.(*)The size of the band is defined by the\n   385→range of temperature deviation that can occur during\n   386→reflow and yield high quality results. This is indicated\n   387→by the crosshatched area shown in the figure. In the\n   388→example, the profile band size is indicated as 25°C.\n   389→The actual size of the band will vary depending upon\n   390→solderpaste, component type, and circuit board material.\n   391→\n   392→BURNT BOARDS,\n   393→DEWET SOLDER\n   394→\n   395→fi\n   396→COLD SOLDER\n   397→JOINTS, DULL\n   398→SOLDER JOINTS\n   399→(TOO COLD)\n   400→\n   401→SOLDER SPATTER\n   402→AND SOLDER BALLS\n   403→CAUSED BY INSUFFICIENT DRYING.\n   404→\n   405→REFLOW SOLDERING DEFECTS\n   406→If the product temperature profile is not maintained\n   407→within the band, a defect will occur. Chip capacitors\n   408→may crack if the rise rate in the preheat zone is excessive. Solder spatter and solder balls may occur if drying\n   409→is excessive or insufficient. If drying is excessive, the\n   410→solder paste may skin over and not allow any of the\n   411→volatiles to escape. As the temperatureof the solderjoint\n   412→continues to heat, the vapor pressure increases until the\n   413→solder joint virtually explodes as the gases escape rapidly. The result is solder spatter and solder balls. If the\n   414→preheat is insufficient, solder spatter and balls may occur\n   415→in the reflow zone when higher heating rates occur. It is\n   416→critical that the paste is dried at low controlled heating\n   417→rates. In the reflow zone, insufficient heat will result in\n   418→non-reflowed or partially reflowed solder joints. The\n   419→\n   420→Section I - Technology\n   421→\n   422→joints will have a dull, rough surface finish in this case.\n   423→Excessive heating in the reflow zone may result in burnt\n   424→boards, damaged components, or possibly solder\n   425→dewetting. In a dewet solderjoint, the solder is reflowed\n   426→but it is pulled away from the pad or the component lead.\n   427→\n   428→DEFINE THE BAND\n   429→The profile band needs to be defined for the given SMT\n   430→assembly. To define this band, one should be aware of\n   431→not only the solder paste requirements, but also any\n   432→specific requirements of components or the circuit board\n   433→material. The process band width is defined as the total\n   434→deviation in temperature that can occur and yield reliable\n   435→results.\n   436→\n   437→OVEN PERFORMANCE\n   438→From a process standpoint,the reflow oven must be able\n   439→to produce consistent results within the reflow profile\n   440→band. The user should be aware of all variables that\n   441→influence the band. These variables may be defined as\n   442→product related and oven related. By far the most critical\n   443→product related non-uniformity is that created by large\n   444→mass differences on the product. Since it is easier to heat\n   445→an area that has no components as compared with an area\n   446→with large components, temperature differentials will\n   447→exist on the product. Machine related non-uniformities\n   448→such as repeatability under product load and edge-tocenter and front-to-back product heating uniformities\n   449→must also be considered. To achieve high quality, low\n   450→defect soldering results, the sum of all the non-uniformities must fall within the defined reflow profile band.\n   451→\n   452→In reality the profile is not a line, but rather\n   453→a range of temperatures all solder joints\n   454→must be exposed.\n   455→The total temperature deviation from\n   456→component thermal mismatch, oven loading, and oven uniformity must be within\n   457→the band.\n   458→\n   459→5\n   460→\n   461→\fWHAT ARE THE CAUSES OF NON-UNIFORM HEATING?\n   462→In order to fully comprehend the capabilityto heat within\n   463→the defined process band, one needs to better understand\n   464→the causes of heating non-uniformities. The three main\n   465→causes of non-uniform heating in the SMT reflow process include mass differential on the product, conveyor\n   466→and heater edge effects, and product loading.\n   467→\n   468→MASS DIFFERENTIAL\n   469→The amount of temperature rise of a product as it travels\n   470→through a heating zone depends upon a number of\n   471→variables. The temperaturerise of an object subjected to\n   472→heat is determined by the following equation:\n   473→\n   474→AT = Q x A X t / (M x Cp)\n   475→WHERE:\n   476→AT = Product Temperature Rise (“C)\n   477→Q = Heat Absorbed (w/cmz)\n   478→A = Exposed Area (cm2)\n   479→t = Heating Dwell Time (seconds)\n   480→M = Mass of Object (kg)\n   481→Cp = Specific Heat (w-sec/kg-”C)\n   482→\n   483→OVEN UNIFORMITY\n   484→A second main cause of non-uniform product heating is\n   485→the oven heating pattern. This can be caused by edge\n   486→effects on the heaters (less heat at the end) or by heat\n   487→sinking of the conveyor system. The edge effect may be\n   488→caused by insufficient airflow around the edges of the\n   489→machine, or by non-uniformities that are inherent in the\n   490→heaters. The heat sinking of the conveyor is really amass\n   491→effect, similar to the one described above. The oven\n   492→uniformity may be measured by thermocoupling a bare\n   493→board over a matrix of points, and running a profile. The\n   494→matrix should include points on the board edges (front,\n   495→back, left, and right), and the center of the board.\n   496→\n   497→OVEN REPEATABILITY\n   498→Repeatability refers to the oven’s capability to repeat a\n   499→given profile. Repeatability is affected by machine\n   500→loading. The figure below shows a typical product\n   501→loading condition. The “Loading Factor” is defined in\n   502→the figure.\n   503→PRODUCT FLOW INTO OVEN\n   504→\n   505→b\n   506→\n   507→The mass (M) times the specificheat (Cp)is often referred\n   508→to as thermal mass. Greater thermal mass requires more\n   509→heat to achieve a given temperature rise. In the figure\n   510→shown below, the area populated with the PLCC’s has\n   511→considerably more thermal mass per unit area than the\n   512→area with the discrete components. This area will be more\n   513→difficult to heat. The non-uniformity caused by thermal\n   514→mass differential may be measured by thermocoupling a\n   515→lead on the largest and smallest device, and running a\n   516→profile.\n   517→HIGH MASS DIFFERENTIAL\n   518→\n   519→J\n   520→mmmm\n   521→mmmm\n   522→mmmm\n   523→mmmm\n   524→\n   525→mmmm\n   526→mmmm\n   527→\n   528→6\n   529→\n   530→L\n   531→\n   532→LOADING FACTOR =\n   533→\n   534→L+s\n   535→\n   536→The higher the value of the loading factor, the more\n   537→difficult it is for any oven to give repeatable results.\n   538→Ovens will generally specify a maximum loading factor\n   539→(ranging from 0.5 to 0.9). The loading factor becomes an\n   540→important consideration when sizing an oven. The nonuniformity caused by repeatability can be measured by\n   541→running a profile on a test board, then load the oven\n   542→down. Periodically, run the test board within the oven\n   543→load and compare with the unloaded profile.\n   544→\n   545→The three main causes of non-uniform heating in\n   546→the SMT reflow process include mass differential\n   547→on the product, conveyor and heater edge effects,\n   548→and product loading.\n   549→\n   550→Section I - Technology\n   551→\n   552→\fWHAT ARE THE MODES OF HEATING?\n   553→There are three different heating modes involved wit\n   554→most SMT reflow processes; convection, conductior\n   555→and infrared radiation (IR). All three of these heatin\n   556→modes occur naturally in our daily lives. Perhaps th\n   557→easiest way to understand each of these heating mode\n   558→is through example.\n   559→\n   560→HEAT FLOWS FROM HOT TO COLD TO HELP\n   561→EQUALIZE TEMPERATURES\n   562→\n   563→CONVECTION\n   564→Convection heat transfer occurs when a fluid (such a\n   565→air, nitrogen, or water) passes over a solid (such as a\n   566→SMT assembly). A cool breeze on a hot sunny da\n   567→provides convective cooling. Hot air from a hair drye\n   568→provides convective heating. Convection heating c\n   569→cooling requires contact of the flow with the solid par\n   570→Only the layer of the flow that is in contact with the p a\n   571→is actually transferring heat.\n   572→\n   573→HOT\n   574→AIR\n   575→FLOW\n   576→\n   577→RADIATION\n   578→Infrared Radiation (IR) occurs when two bodies of different temperature are in sight of each other. The best\n   579→example of IR is the heating of the earth by the sun. IR\n   580→is a non-contact heat transfer.\n   581→\n   582→RADIATION\n   583→\n   584→I\n   585→\n   586→.\n   587→-\n   588→\n   589→2ONVECTION\n   590→\n   591→CONDUCTION\n   592→\n   593→I\n   594→\n   595→\\\n   596→\n   597→COLD OBJECT IS\n   598→HEATED BY CONVECTION\n   599→FROM THE HOT AIR FLOW\n   600→\n   601→CONDUCTION\n   602→Conduction heat transfer occurs when two solid masse\n   603→of different temperature are in contact with each other\n   604→A good example is when a pan is placed on an electril\n   605→burner. Most of the heat is transferred to the pan by thl\n   606→contact between the pan and the burner. Conduction alsc\n   607→occurs within the same mass if a temperature differen\n   608→tial exists within the mass.\n   609→\n   610→Referring to a system as 100% convection or\n   611→100% IR is incorrect. A system may be dominated\n   612→by convection or IR, but the other heat transfer is\n   613→always present in signijicant quantities.\n   614→\n   615→Section I - Technology\n   616→\n   617→In SMT reflow applications (with the exception of vapor\n   618→phase), convection, conduction, and IR all play a role in\n   619→the heating process. Referring to a system as 100%\n   620→convection or 100% IR is incorrect. A system may be\n   621→dominated by convection or IR, but the other heat transfer is always present. IR dominant systems usually range\n   622→in the convection/IR ratio of 60/40 to 40/60. Convection\n   623→dominant systems have ratios in the range of 70/30 to\n   624→80/20. Conduction heat transfer occurs more within the\n   625→product during the heating process. Heat is transferred\n   626→through the product to help minimize hot or cold spots.\n   627→\n   628→7\n   629→\n   630→\fHOW DOES INFRARED HEATING WORK?\n   631→Infrared (IR) heat transfer occurs when two objects at\n   632→different temperatures are in sight of each other. The\n   633→heat is transferred by electromagnetic waves of 0.781000 micron wavelengths. All objects emit some level\n   634→of infrared. The quantity of infrared energy emitted and\n   635→the wavelength of the emission are both dependent upon\n   636→the absolute temperature of the object. As the source\n   637→temperature increases, the heat transfer output increases\n   638→exponentiallyto the fourth power. Increasing the source\n   639→temperature results in shorter wavelengths. Decreasing\n   640→the source temperature results in longer wavelengths. In\n   641→SMT reflow the amount of heat transfer is important, so\n   642→as not to exceed the 2OC/sec rate. When operating at\n   643→these low heating rates all types of radiant emitters\n   644→(lamps, panels, tubes) operate in the medium to long\n   645→wavelength ranges.\n   646→\n   647→The geometric view factor “V” is the fraction of energy\n   648→that leaves the source that hits the target. In SMTreflow,\n   649→the oven chamber designs yield very high view factors\n   650→in the range of 0.90 to 0.95. An important aspect of the\n   651→view factor comes in product design. If two very large\n   652→components are in close proximity to each other, the\n   653→view factor to a solder joint between them is decreased,\n   654→which makes it more difficult. Refer to figure.\n   655→f\n   656→\n   657→IRSOURCE\n   658→\n   659→/\n   660→\n   661→THIS AREA IS SHADED BY\n   662→ADJACENT COMPONENTS\n   663→RESULTING IN A LOWER VIEW\n   664→FACTOR\n   665→\n   666→INFRARED HEATING EQUATION\n   667→In order to understand what parameters are important in\n   668→infrared heating, one can consider the general equation\n   669→for heat transfer between the heat source and the object\n   670→being heated. Again, the purpose is not to memorize the\n   671→equation, but rather to point out the significance of what\n   672→is involved. The general equation for heat transfer is as\n   673→follows:\n   674→\n   675→Q = V x Es x At x K x (Ts4 - Tt4)\n   676→WHERE:\n   677→Q = Infrared heat transfer (w/cm2)\n   678→V = Geometric view factor (0-1)\n   679→Es = Emissivity factor of the source (0-1)\n   680→At = Absorptivity factor of the target (0-1)\n   681→K = Stefan-Boltzmann Constant\n   682→(5.67 x\n   683→w/cm2/”K4)\n   684→Ts = Source temperature (OK)\n   685→Tt = Target temperature (OK)\n   686→\n   687→Absorption characteristics of solder\n   688→paste and circuit board material makes\n   689→IR an efficient means of heating.\n   690→\n   691→8\n   692→\n   693→The source emissivity and the target absorptivity factors\n   694→are in the range on 0.90 to 0.95 for most SMT applications. Solder paste is an excellent absorber of infrared\n   695→energy. Shiny gold components may be difficult to heat\n   696→as they tend to be reflective. Most often, however, the\n   697→board material, solder paste, and the components all\n   698→absorb quite well. The figure below shows the spectral\n   699→absorptivity for an RMA solder paste.(3)\n   700→I\n   701→loooo\n   702→\n   703→!\n   704→\n   705→97.39\n   706→\n   707→96’70\n   708→96.04\n   709→95.38\n   710→\n   711→t\n   712→I4 I\n   713→\n   714→I!\n   715→\n   716→1\n   717→\n   718→1\n   719→\n   720→1\n   721→\n   722→1\n   723→\n   724→1\n   725→\n   726→1\n   727→\n   728→l\n   729→\n   730→I\n   731→\n   732→Control of infrared is generally done by controlling the\n   733→source temperature. Providing that the overall source\n   734→emission can be regulated, IR can provide high levels of\n   735→repeatability.\n   736→\n   737→Section I - Technology\n   738→\n   739→\fHOW DOES CONVECTION HEATING WORK?\n   740→Convection heat transfer occurs when a fluid at a given\n   741→temperature contacts a solid mass at a different temperature. If the fluid is hotter than the mass, the mass will be\n   742→heated. If the fluid is cooler than the mass, the mass will\n   743→be cooled. Perhaps the easiest way to understand convection is to look at the convection equation and note what\n   744→is important and what is not important.\n   745→\n   746→CONVECTION EQUATION\n   747→\n   748→very cold. That is because the higher velocity wind has\n   749→a higher convective coefficient, and thus removes heat\n   750→more effectively from your skin. In SMT reflow applications, increasing the velocity of the flow also improves\n   751→the amount of convection heat transfer. There are practical limits to this, as too high of velocity may cause\n   752→components to shift around. The flow direction also has\n   753→a significant impact on “Q’. Parallel flow convection\n   754→is shown below:\n   755→\n   756→The following is the general equation for convection\n   757→heating or cooling:\n   758→\n   759→I\n   760→\n   761→Q=HxAT\n   762→\n   763→FLOW DIRECTION 4\n   764→\n   765→WHERE:\n   766→Q = Heat absorbed per unit area (w/cm2)\n   767→H = Convective film coefficient\n   768→(W/cm2-”C)\n   769→AT = Temperature difference between\n   770→fluid and object (“C)\n   771→\n   772→The amount of heat transfer “Q’ can be modified by\n   773→either changing the convective coefficient “H’ or the\n   774→temperature difference “AT”. Increasing the source\n   775→temperature will increase the temperature difference,\n   776→and thus the amount of heat transfer “Q’ will increase\n   777→proportionally.\n   778→The convective coefficient “H’ can have a significant\n   779→impact on the heat transfer “Q”. The value of “H” is\n   780→related to a number of variables, including velocity of\n   781→the flow and angle of attack of the flow.\n   782→Increasing the flow velocity will increase the value of\n   783→“H”. For example, when one is outside in the wind, a\n   784→low velocity cool breeze on a warm day will feel quite\n   785→nice. However, a strong wind on that same day may feel\n   786→\n   787→~\n   788→\n   789→~~\n   790→\n   791→BOUNDARY LAYER FORMATION\n   792→CAUSES STAGNANT FLOW NEAR\n   793→BOARD, WHICH REDUCES\n   794→HEAT TRANSFER\n   795→\n   796→Note that in the parallel flow diagram, there is an area\n   797→referred to as the “boundary layer”. In the boundary\n   798→layer, there is no flow motion, which causes convective\n   799→heat transfer to be very low. It is beneficial to break up\n   800→the boundary layer to allow the flow to transfer heat\n   801→freely to the object. The diagram shown below shows\n   802→perpendicular flow convection:\n   803→PERPENDICULAR FLOW\n   804→FLOW DIRECTION\n   805→\n   806→t\n   807→\n   808→t\n   809→\n   810→t\n   811→\n   812→NO BOUNDARY\n   813→LAYER\n   814→\n   815→NO BOUNDARY LAYER FORMATION\n   816→ENHANCES CONVECTIVE HEAT\n   817→TRANSFER\n   818→\n   819→~~\n   820→\n   821→Perpendicular flow convection breaks\n   822→up surface thermal boundary layers,\n   823→which enhances heat transfer.\n   824→\n   825→Section I - Technology\n   826→\n   827→-\n   828→\n   829→PARALLEL FLOW WIBOUNDARY LAYER\n   830→\n   831→In the diagram showing perpendicular flow, note that\n   832→there is no boundary layer. This is the reason that\n   833→perpendicular flow yields better convective heating or\n   834→cooling results.\n   835→\n   836→9\n   837→\n   838→\fWHAT ARE THE BENEFITS OF IR?\n   839→In SMT reflow applications, there is no such thing as\n   840→pure convection or pure IR. The only way to achieve a\n   841→100% convection system would be to have gas flowing\n   842→over the product with no chamber walls. Of course this\n   843→in not practical. To have a 100%IR system, the chamber\n   844→would need to be operated in a vacuum. Again, this is not\n   845→the case. However, some systems are more dominated by\n   846→convection, while others utilize IR as the primary heat\n   847→source. For the sake of simplicity this text will refer to a\n   848→convection dominated machine as a convection machine, and an IR dominate machine as an IR machine.\n   849→\n   850→...CONVECTION?\n   851→\n   852→is through the use of recirculation. In a recirculation\n   853→loop, the process gas from a zone is evacuated, then\n   854→reheated and re-input into the heating chamber. The\n   855→limitation to recirculating is that high nitrogen purity\n   856→levels are not typically attainable. There is also a concern\n   857→that if the gas is recirculated excessively, it may affect\n   858→soldering quality, as the atmosphere surrounding the\n   859→product becomes contaminated with process by-products. Convection increases the chance of solder wicking,\n   860→as typically the lead is heated faster than the circuit board.\n   861→\n   862→IR\n   863→CONVECTION\n   864→Both convection and IR have their unique advantages\n   865→and limitations. In convective systems, typically the\n   866→temperature of the flow is close to the desired temperature of the product in a given zone. This has the\n   867→advantage in that the product temperature will never\n   868→exceed the gas flow temperature. This is useful when\n   869→heating a product that has a large difference in the mass\n   870→of the components and distribution. In this case, the\n   871→larger componentswill continue to heat, while the smaller\n   872→components will not overheat. Convection heat transfer\n   873→is not thermally efficient. Air (or nitrogen) has a very low\n   874→heat capacity, which means that high volumes of flow\n   875→may be required to transfer the heat. One way to improve\n   876→efficiency and reduce the amount of process gas required\n   877→\n   878→CONVECTION\n   879→\n   880→10\n   881→\n   882→IR systems have the distinct advantage of high heating\n   883→efficienciesand low process gas flows required to reflow\n   884→solder. IR systems are normally very repeatable, and\n   885→have the capability to transfer high quantities of heat as\n   886→needed. This becomes quite useful when reflowing very\n   887→dense SMT assemblies or very heavy boards. The\n   888→limitation with IR is that a high temperature differential\n   889→between the source and the product is required to transfer\n   890→the heat. Products with high component mass differential do not heat uniformly. They must be run quite slowly\n   891→to accomplish reflow. IR systems are successful at\n   892→reflowing products with component sizes up to and\n   893→including 84 pin PLCC’s. Large connectors and pin grid\n   894→arrays are quite difficult to reflow on an IR dominated\n   895→system.\n   896→\n   897→IR\n   898→\n   899→Less sensitive to shading\n   900→\n   901→Control source temperature only\n   902→\n   903→Heats more uniformly\n   904→\n   905→High efficiency (60-70%)\n   906→\n   907→AT (Source-target) is small\n   908→\n   909→Easy to control rate of rise\n   910→\n   911→Low thermal mass of air requires\n   912→high flow rates to transfer heat\n   913→\n   914→More sensitive to shading\n   915→\n   916→Inefficient (20 to 30%)\n   917→\n   918→Less uniform high mass differences\n   919→\n   920→Will heat leads first, increasing\n   921→chance of solder wicking.\n   922→\n   923→AT (Source-target) is large\n   924→\n   925→Section I - Technology\n   926→\n   927→\fWHAT ARE THE BENEFITS OF A NITROGEN ATMOSPHERE?\n   928→Reflow soldering in a nitrogen atmosphere is becoming\n   929→a significant process consideration. The issue is really\n   930→not the ability to reflow in nitrogen, but rather the ability\n   931→to reflow in the absence of oxygen. Heating solder in\n   932→the presence of oxygen will create oxides, which are\n   933→generally non-solderable surfaces. In past days of reflow\n   934→soldering,the solderpaste consisted of aggressiveenough\n   935→fluxes to remove oxides during the reflow process. The\n   936→future trend is moving away from strong fluxes in the\n   937→paste. The future trends include two major process\n   938→changes that are driving the need for reflow in nitrogen\n   939→atmospheres; fine pitch soldering and no-clean fluxes.\n   940→\n   941→FINE PITCH REFLOW SOLDERING\n   942→Fine pitch soldering presents many challenges. In order\n   943→to obtain satisfactory paste print definition, the particle\n   944→sizes of tin and lead are becoming smaller. As the\n   945→particles become smaller, the ratio of surface area to\n   946→volume of the particles increases. This can be shown by:\n   947→\n   948→RATIO = (n x D*) /(1/6 x n x D3)\n   949→RATIO = 6 / D\n   950→WHERE:\n   951→D = Particle Diameter\n   952→\n   953→NO-CLEAN SOLDERING\n   954→The second technology change that is driving the need\n   955→towards nitrogen atmospheres is the greater usage of noclean pastes. The rapid trend away from solvent cleaning\n   956→is creating this and other needs. No-clean soldering\n   957→requires a tight control of the process. All of the components and boards must be highly solderable. The reflow\n   958→process must not add any significant oxidation to the\n   959→process. The fluxes used are very weak, and do not have\n   960→aggressive oxide cleaning action. In addition, there is no\n   961→cleaning that would remove any solder balls from the\n   962→product. These solder balls eventually can create short\n   963→circuits on the product if they are not removed. Reflowing\n   964→in a high purity nitrogen environment reduces the possibility of creating solder balls.\n   965→A good question to ask is “How pure a nitrogen environment is required?” A recent study (4) by Colin Lea of the\n   966→National Physics Laboratory in the United Kingdom was\n   967→conducted to determine the affect of nitrogen purity on\n   968→the process. The conclusions drawn in the study are\n   969→listed below:\n   970→\n   971→I PURITY LEVEL I\n   972→\n   973→DESCRIPTION\n   974→\n   975→Significant reduction\n   976→in board discoloration\n   977→\n   978→10,000ppm\n   979→\n   980→~\n   981→\n   982→FOR THE SAME VOLUME (OR MASS) OF SOLDER, THERE IS\n   983→GREATER SURFACE AREA WITH SMALLER PARTICLES THAN\n   984→WITH LARGER PARTICLES.\n   985→\n   986→Thus, as particles sizes decrease, the ratio increases.\n   987→With more available surface area in the solder paste,\n   988→there is a greater tendency towards oxidation. Keep in\n   989→mind that oxidation is a function of the metals involved,\n   990→the temperatures of the metal, the exposed surface area,\n   991→and the amount of oxygen present. The only variable\n   992→that one can control is the amount of oxygen present.\n   993→This can only be done through proper oven design.\n   994→\n   995→Section I - Technology\n   996→\n   997→Ease of cleaning flux\n   998→residues (RMA)\n   999→\n  1000→500 ppm\n  1001→\n  1002→Reduction in solder\n  1003→balling (RMA)\n  1004→\n  1005→200 ppm\n  1006→\n  1007→Reflow of low-residue\n  1008→solder paste\n  1009→\n  1010→< 100 ppm\n  1011→\n  1012→The paper presents a number of excellent graphs that\n  1013→show the affect of nitrogen purity on parameters including flux spread, wetting force, wetting time, solder balls,\n  1014→cleaning, residue, board discoloration, and micro-structure. It is recommended reading if your process may\n  1015→require the use of nitrogen. You must determine the\n  1016→nitrogen requirements for your process. The benefits of\n  1017→using nitrogen definitely exist, however you must determine if the additional cost of nitrogen is worthwhile for\n  1018→your process.\n  1019→\n  1020→11\n  1021→\n  1022→\f1 SECTION II - EQUIPMENT SELECTION I\n  1023→WHAT ARE THE STEPS IN THE SELECTION PROCESS?\n  1024→When selecting reflow equipment,it is important to take\n  1025→into consideration all aspects that may affect the process\n  1026→and production. Try to avoid becoming too focussed on\n  1027→one issue, such as populated uniformity, as it may not\n  1028→lead you to the optimum decision. The recommended\n  1029→steps in the selection process are described in the following paragraphs.\n  1030→\n  1031→DEFINE PROCESS BAND WIDTH\n  1032→The first step is to define the acceptable process profile\n  1033→band width for your SMT assemblies. Try to be as\n  1034→accurate as possible when defining the band. If the band\n  1035→is too restrictive, some aspect of production will be\n  1036→negatively affected. If the band is too loose, a defect will\n  1037→show up in the process. The band width should be\n  1038→defined as a range of temperatures (\"C) that the entire\n  1039→profile must fall within. Refer to page 4 for a more\n  1040→detailed description.\n  1041→\n  1042→DEFINE HEATING NON-UNIFORMITIES\n  1043→The next step is to define causes of heating non-uniformities that may occur in the process. These non-uniformities are caused by mass differential, oven uniformity,\n  1044→and oven repeatability under production loading conditions. A set of tests must be conducted with the desired\n  1045→product on the oven in question. The sum of the nonuniformities (\"C) must be less than the profile band width\n  1046→defined in the first step. Refer to page 5 for a more\n  1047→detailed description.\n  1048→\n  1049→SIZE THE OVEN PROPERLY\n  1050→The oven must be sized to handle the production rate of\n  1051→the pick-and-placeequipment. The calculationis shown\n  1052→below:\n  1053→LINE SPEED - BOARDS PER MINUTE X LENGTH PER BOARD\n  1054→(MINIMUM) LOAD FACTOR\n  1055→\n  1056→Operating at a conveyor speed greater than the calculated\n  1057→speed ensures that no product jams will occur on the\n  1058→onload area of the reflow oven.\n  1059→\n  1060→Try to avoid becoming too focussed on one\n  1061→issue, such as populated uniformity, as it may\n  1062→not lead you to the optimum decision.\n  1063→\n  1064→12\n  1065→\n  1066→In order for the oven to be properly sized, the process\n  1067→speed defined below must be greater than the minimum\n  1068→calculated line speed. This will ensure that bottlenecks\n  1069→will not occur at the reflow oven. The process speed\n  1070→should be determined through testing or by the following\n  1071→equation:\n  1072→PROCESS SPEED -\n  1073→\n  1074→OVEN CHAMBER HEATED LENGTH\n  1075→PROCESS DWELL TIME\n  1076→\n  1077→DEFINE PROCESS GAS\n  1078→If a nitrogen atmosphere is required for solderability or\n  1079→no-clean processing (see page lo), one needs to consider\n  1080→the purity required and the gas consumption. Be careful\n  1081→how the purity is measured, such that it is not measured\n  1082→at the gas inlet.\n  1083→\n  1084→REVIEW CONVEYOR DESIGN\n  1085→The conveying mechanism is perhaps the most overlooked aspect of equipment selection. One needs to\n  1086→define whether a mesh conveyor or an edge type is\n  1087→required. Typically edge conveyors are used for in-line\n  1088→processing or if double-sided assembly is required. The\n  1089→most important issue with edge conveyors is the ability\n  1090→for the conveyor to remain straight to avoid dropping\n  1091→product.\n  1092→\n  1093→RELIABILITY/SERVICEABILITY\n  1094→Once a machine is placed into service, it is critical that it\n  1095→remains operational. Evaluate the product design from a\n  1096→structural as well as a functional standpoint. Is the oven\n  1097→designed to withstand the rigors of production? Review\n  1098→the product manuals, drawings, and spare parts lists.\n  1099→Find out where the nearest servicing center is located. Is\n  1100→training available? These are the questions that must be\n  1101→asked during the selection process.\n  1102→\n  1103→INTERFACING REQUIREMENTS\n  1104→Conveyor and control interfacing requirements may also\n  1105→be an important selection issue. Make sure that the edge\n  1106→conveyor fixed rail justification is the same as the pickand-place equipment. Ask questions about networking\n  1107→machines to a host and product flow control schemes if\n  1108→needed.\n  1109→\n  1110→Section I1 - Equipment Selection\n  1111→\n  1112→\fHOW CAN YOU EVALUATE COSTS?\n  1113→The selection steps listed on the previous page should\n  1114→all be considered when comparing equipment. When\n  1115→multiple ovens pass the selection criteria listed, then a\n  1116→proper cost evaluation may be used to compare ovens.\n  1117→This is especially important when comparing ovens that\n  1118→utilize different heating technologies, as first cost and\n  1119→operating costs can be significantly different. This\n  1120→section describes various costs that may be included in\n  1121→the analysis.\n  1122→\n  1123→EQUIPMENT COST\n  1124→Often, the initial cost of the equipment is the only cost\n  1125→considered when comparing equipment. It is certainly\n  1126→important, although it is only a part of the total cost\n  1127→picture. To make a fair comparison, all costs should be\n  1128→analyzed with a common basis, such as annual cost. As\n  1129→an example let’s look at amachine that costs $50,000and\n  1130→has an expected life of 5 years, with an interest rate of\n  1131→10%. Assume the value of the equipment is zero at the\n  1132→end of five years. The annualized cost of the equipment\n  1133→is equal to the initial cost times the Capital Recover\n  1134→Factor. (3 For an interest rate of 10% with a period of\n  1135→5 years, the CRF value is equal to 0.2638. The annualized cost is then equal to:\n  1136→\n  1137→MAINTENANCE AND SPARE PARTS COSTS\n  1138→The maintenance and spare parts costs should be included in the analysis. This may be the most difficult to\n  1139→predict, but it should be included.\n  1140→\n  1141→-\n  1142→\n  1143→SUMMARY\n  1144→\n  1145→-\n  1146→\n  1147→An example of an annual cost analysis is shown in the\n  1148→table below, comparing a convection (hot gas) machine\n  1149→to an IR machine. The values used are not specific to any\n  1150→vendor’s equipment or products, merely typical values\n  1151→from the market. The total annual estimated costs reveal\n  1152→the importance of including the operational costs in the\n  1153→analysis.\n  1154→HOT GAS\n  1155→\n  1156→DESCRIPTION\n  1157→\n  1158→GAS USAGE\n  1159→POWERUSAGE\n  1160→\n  1161→+-I\n  1162→\n  1163→IR\n  1164→\n  1165→I 1800CFH I 1200CFH I\n  1166→\n  1167→I\n  1168→\n  1169→20KW\n  1170→\n  1171→I\n  1172→\n  1173→I\n  1174→\n  1175→12KW\n  1176→\n  1177→HOURSEEAR\n  1178→\n  1179→2080 HRS\n  1180→\n  1181→2080 HRS\n  1182→\n  1183→INITIAL COST\n  1184→\n  1185→$50,000\n  1186→\n  1187→$60,000\n  1188→\n  1189→GAS COST\n  1190→PER 1000 CFH\n  1191→\n  1192→$5.00\n  1193→\n  1194→$5.00\n  1195→\n  1196→POWER COST\n  1197→PER KW-HR\n  1198→\n  1199→$0.10\n  1200→\n  1201→$0.10\n  1202→\n  1203→CRF\n  1204→(FROM ABOVE)\n  1205→\n  1206→0.2638\n  1207→\n  1208→0.2638\n  1209→\n  1210→EQUIPMENT\n  1211→\n  1212→$13,190\n  1213→\n  1214→$15,828\n  1215→\n  1216→When multiple ovens pass the selection criteria\n  1217→listed, then aproper cost evaluation may be used\n  1218→to compare ovens.\n  1219→\n  1220→GAS COST\n  1221→\n  1222→$18,720\n  1223→\n  1224→$12,480\n  1225→\n  1226→POWER COST\n  1227→\n  1228→$4,160\n  1229→\n  1230→$2,496\n  1231→\n  1232→The importance of factoring in operating costs\n  1233→should be noted.\n  1234→\n  1235→MAINTENANCE\n  1236→\n  1237→$ 1,000\n  1238→\n  1239→$1,000\n  1240→\n  1241→TOTAL\n  1242→\n  1243→$37,070\n  1244→\n  1245→$31,804\n  1246→\n  1247→~\n  1248→\n  1249→Annual Cost = $50,000 x 0.2638\n  1250→= $13,190\n  1251→\n  1252→OPERATING COST\n  1253→The operating cost needs to include the electrical power\n  1254→to operate the machine and also the cost of any process\n  1255→gases (such as nitrogen). If there are other pertinent\n  1256→operating costs, include them in this analysis.\n  1257→\n  1258→Section I1 - Equipment Selection\n  1259→\n  1260→-\n  1261→\n  1262→~\n  1263→\n  1264→13\n  1265→\n  1266→\fSELECTION CRITERIA CHART\n  1267→PROCESS VARIABLE\n  1268→\n  1269→VENDOR A\n  1270→\n  1271→VENDOR C\n  1272→\n  1273→VENDOR B\n  1274→\n  1275→PROCESS BAND WIDTH REQUIRED (\"C)\n  1276→HEAT1NG NON-UNIFORMlTlES\n  1277→MASS DIFFERENTIAL (\"C)\n  1278→OVEN UNIFORMITY (\"C)\n  1279→\n  1280→I\n  1281→\n  1282→REPEATABILITY (\"C)\n  1283→\n  1284→I\n  1285→\n  1286→1\n  1287→\n  1288→I\n  1289→\n  1290→MAXIMUM LOADING FACTOR\n  1291→I\n  1292→\n  1293→PROCESS SPEED\n  1294→PROCESS GAS TYPE\n  1295→DRYOUT ZONE PURITY (PPM)\n  1296→\n  1297→I\n  1298→\n  1299→PROCESS GAS FLOW\n  1300→POWER REQUIRED\n  1301→UNDER PRODUCTION LOAD (KW)\n  1302→\n  1303→CONVEYOR TYPE\n  1304→CONVEYOR STRAIGHTNESS\n  1305→(IF EDGE)\n  1306→CONVEYOR DIRECTION/\n  1307→RAIL JUSTIFICATION\n  1308→GENERAL MACHINE QUALITY\n  1309→RATING (1-10)\n  1310→MANUALS AND DOCUMENTATION\n  1311→RATING (1-10)\n  1312→SERVICING LOCATION\n  1313→COMPUTER INTERFACE AVAILABLE\n  1314→\n  1315→ANNUAL\n  1316→14\n  1317→\n  1318→COST TO OPERATE\n  1319→\n  1320→Section I1 - Equipment Selection\n  1321→\n  1322→\f! SECTION III - RESEARCH, INC. EQUIPMENT TECHNOLOGY~\n  1323→CONVECTION OVEN THERMAL CONTROL\n  1324→Convection oven thermal control requires tight control\n  1325→of two variables; temperature and flow. In order to\n  1326→maintain constant heat flux to the product, it is critical\n  1327→that both these variables are controlled. Research, Inc.’s\n  1328→MICRI-FLO XG forced convection reflow system will\n  1329→be used as an example. The temperature and flow control\n  1330→are defined specific to this system.\n  1331→\n  1332→CONVECTION OVEN DESCRIPTION\n  1333→Temperature control of the system must consider both\n  1334→the gas and the surrounding wall temperature. Remember, even in a convection dominant heating system, the\n  1335→walls radiate IR energy worth about 20%-30% of the\n  1336→total heat flux of the system. The figure below shows\n  1337→the heater cavity flow diagram for the MICRI-FLO XG.\n  1338→The reflow and dryout zones each receive input gas\n  1339→(air or nitrogen) through a series of flow meters. This\n  1340→controlled flow enters a diffusion plenum behind the\n  1341→heating plenum to distribute the flow. The flow then\n  1342→enters a second plenum, where the gas is preheated by a\n  1343→series of heaters. The heaters consist of a wound nickelchromium wire encased in a stainless steel jacket. The\n  1344→\n  1345→heaters are bent in a “U” shape such that easy removal\n  1346→can occur from one side of the machine. The gas then\n  1347→enters the process chamber through an array of holes.\n  1348→The product is heated from a combination of the hot gas\n  1349→and the hot chamber surroundings. A control thermocouple is mounted in each zone to maintain cavity\n  1350→repeatability. A PID (Proportional - Integral - Derivative) control loop is used to maintain the cavity at\n  1351→constant conditions.\n  1352→The flow is removed from the reflow area by two exhaust\n  1353→ducts located on each side of the reflow zone. This flow\n  1354→is pulled from the zones using a high temperature, low\n  1355→flow recirculating blower. The flow is pushed by the\n  1356→blower through the preheater plenum arrangement. The\n  1357→temperature control is accomplished in the same way as\n  1358→the dryout and reflow zone.\n  1359→The flow that heats the product (in all zones) is forced\n  1360→through the walls in an array of holes to break up the\n  1361→boundary layers on the product. The perpendicular flow\n  1362→arrangement shown on page 8 is used to optimize heat\n  1363→transfer.\n  1364→\n  1365→GAS FLOW INPUT FROM\n  1366→AIR OR NITROGEN SOURCE\n  1367→\n  1368→Convection oven thermal control requires tight\n  1369→control of two variables; temperature andflow.\n  1370→In order to maintain constant heat flux to the\n  1371→product, it is critical that both these variables\n  1372→are controlled.\n  1373→\n  1374→II4\n  1375→\n  1376→Y\n  1377→\n  1378→GAS FLOW INPUT FROM AIR OR NITROGEN SOURCE\n  1379→\n  1380→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology\n  1381→\n  1382→15\n  1383→\n  1384→\fCONVECTION OVEN WITH VITROGEN ATMOSPHERE\n  1385→Convection ovens are a greater challenge to inert for\n  1386→several reasons. First, the amount of flow that would be\n  1387→required to transfer 80% of the energy by convection is\n  1388→extremely high, and cost prohibitive. In order to minimize this, recirculation ducts are added, which result in\n  1389→the degradation of the purity of the source flow entering\n  1390→a given zone. Higher velocities and turbulent flow are\n  1391→also required, which can result in a greater opportunity\n  1392→for oxygen to enter the system. Third, the pressure in the\n  1393→heater cavity is positive in some areas, and negative in\n  1394→others. Balancing this flow becomes adifficult task. The\n  1395→job of inerting a convection oven is not impossible.\n  1396→However, it should be noted that purity levels and flow\n  1397→rates are less optimum than with an IR based machine.\n  1398→\n  1399→etary gas plenum distribution system. The pressure in the\n  1400→entry and exit purge sections are maintained at sufficient\n  1401→levels to minimize oxygen infiltration. Flow meter\n  1402→controlled venturi exhausts are used to balance the flow\n  1403→in the system and to remove process volatiles.\n  1404→In order to maintain the best purity levels in the zones\n  1405→where the product temperature is greater than 150°C,\n  1406→pure nitrogen is delivered to the reflow and dryout\n  1407→zone. This is a key point, as this ensures that the product\n  1408→experiences a nearly pure environment at its most critical stages (dryout and reflow). The recirculated gas\n  1409→from these zones is reheated and used in the preheat zone.\n  1410→Here, purity is not as critical, so the use of recirculated\n  1411→gas is not detrimental to the process.\n  1412→\n  1413→CHAMBER DESCRIPTION\n  1414→Many of the same principles apply for inerting when\n  1415→comparing a convectionoven to an IR oven. The MZCRZFLO XG convection oven shown below utilizes a very\n  1416→similar scheme to the 4470XN IR oven. The gas is\n  1417→brought into each zone very uniformly using the propri-\n  1418→\n  1419→Purity levels in the dryout and reflow zone can be\n  1420→maintained at 35 PPM over incoming purity levels at\n  1421→flow rates of 1800 cubic feet per hour (5 1 cubic meters\n  1422→per hour).\n  1423→\n  1424→GAS FLOW\n  1425→INPUT FROM\n  1426→N2 OR AIR\n  1427→SOURCE\n  1428→\n  1429→GAS FLOW\n  1430→INPUT FROM\n  1431→N2 OR AIR\n  1432→SOURCE\n  1433→\n  1434→16\n  1435→\n  1436→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology\n  1437→\n  1438→\fIR/CONVECTION OVEN CLOSED LOOP CONTROL\n  1439→One of the key oven performance issues is the ability to\n  1440→repeat a product temperature profile. Repeatability is\n  1441→influenced most by product loading. Heavy or nonvariable loading can result in the oven incorrectly compensating, resulting in a loss of repeatability. High levels\n  1442→of repeatability can be achieved only if all the components of heat transfer are maintained constant.\n  1443→\n  1444→LAMP DEFINITION\n  1445→The closed loop heat flux control method used in a\n  1446→Research, Inc. lamp based oven will be used as an\n  1447→example of one type of control. The primary heat source\n  1448→in this system is the tungsten filament (T3) lamp. The T3\n  1449→lamp consists of a tungsten filament encased in a sealed\n  1450→quartz envelope. The envelope is filled with an inert gas\n  1451→to keep the filament from oxidizing and failing. The\n  1452→lamps are rated for 5000 hours when operated at 100%\n  1453→capacity. In SMT applications, the lamp power never\n  1454→exceeds 20% capacity, resulting in lamp life in excess of\n  1455→20,000 hours. At typical operating setpoints of 5-15%,\n  1456→the heat transfer to the product is controlled such that\n  1457→heating rates are under 2”C/second,and the IR emission\n  1458→is in the medium to long wavelength range.\n  1459→The greatest advantage that T3 lamps have is their rapid\n  1460→response capability. Within 1to 2 seconds after a voltage\n  1461→change occurs, the lamp is at its new temperature equilibrium. This rapid response is ideal for closed loop\n  1462→control, as energy can be replaced in the heating chamber\n  1463→instantly after its removal.\n  1464→\n  1465→RAPID-RESPONSE (EXPOSED-TIP)\n  1466→THERMOCOUPLE\n  1467→\n  1468→1\n  1469→\n  1470→PRODUCT\n  1471→FLOW\n  1472→\n  1473→-”‘7\\\n  1474→\n  1475→R E F L F\n  1476→\n  1477→JON.\n  1478→\n  1479→VECTION\n  1480→\n  1481→$\n  1482→\n  1483→RE-EMITTED\n  1484→IR\n  1485→\n  1486→\\P\n  1487→\n  1488→PRODUCT\n  1489→\n  1490→Accurate contol is not achieved by maintaining only one\n  1491→of the components constant, but rather the sum of all the\n  1492→thermal energies involved. The lamp IR component is\n  1493→regulated by the SCRpower controlled system. The wall\n  1494→and convective components are collectively monitored\n  1495→by the bulk chamber temperature measured with a thermocouple probe. There are two reference setpoints;\n  1496→percent voltage for the lamp, and the bulk chamber\n  1497→temperature. The bulk chamber setpoint drives the\n  1498→equation. If the chamber is at the correct temperature,\n  1499→then the lamp setpoints will be at their setpoints. If the\n  1500→chamber is too hot, then the lamps will decrease in\n  1501→intensity. If the chamber is too cool, the lamps will\n  1502→operate at a level greater than their setpoint. The lamp\n  1503→power is modified only enough to compensate for the\n  1504→bulk chamber heat transfer deviation. In doing so, the\n  1505→total heat flux to the product is maintained. The correct\n  1506→compensation will occur providing the bulk chamber\n  1507→temperature is within k 25°C. Product temperature\n  1508→repeatability o f f 2.5”C will occur if the bulk chamber\n  1509→is within this defined band.\n  1510→\n  1511→CONTROL DEFINITION\n  1512→In order for the product profile to be repeatable, the total\n  1513→heat flux to the product must be held constant. The total\n  1514→heat flux to the product is the sum of IR from the lamp and\n  1515→wall plus the convective component. Refer to the figure.\n  1516→\n  1517→Within 1 to 2 seconds after a voltage change\n  1518→occurs, the lamp is at its new temperature\n  1519→equilibrium. This rapid response is ideal for\n  1520→closed loop control, as energy can be replaced\n  1521→in the heating chamber instantly after its\n  1522→removal.\n  1523→\n  1524→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology\n  1525→\n  1526→The ability to operate within this wide band has two\n  1527→significant advantages. The first is that the oven may\n  1528→be loaded down to levels up to 80% load. The second is\n  1529→that profile changeover times are reduced significantly.\n  1530→Loading may begin when all oven temperatures are\n  1531→within f 25°C of setpoint. This requires far less time to\n  1532→achieve the “ready” state than if the “ready” process\n  1533→window was f 3”C,which is often the case inreflow oven\n  1534→operation. Warm-up times of less than 15minutes occur.\n  1535→Profile changeover times are 3 to 5 minutes for profile\n  1536→increases of under 40”C,and under 10minutes for profile\n  1537→decreases of under 40°C. Even in the worst case of going\n  1538→from a reflow profile to an adhesive cure profile, the\n  1539→changeover time is under 15 minutes.\n  1540→\n  1541→17\n  1542→\n  1543→\fIR/CONVECTION OVEN WITH NITROGEN ATMOSPHERE\n  1544→Infrared ovens do not require high quantities of flow to\n  1545→accomplish reflow. The predominant heat transfer occurs with IR, which does not require any flow to transfer\n  1546→the heat. The flow in the oven is used to provide some of\n  1547→the benefits of convection, which includes improving\n  1548→populated board uniformity and volatile removal. The\n  1549→amount of flow in the oven is more related to the quantity\n  1550→required to accomplish a high purity nitrogen environment, which is considerably less than in the case of a\n  1551→convection oven.\n  1552→\n  1553→NITROGEN OVEN DESCRIPTION\n  1554→The 4470XN from Research, Inc. is an example of an IR\n  1555→dominant oven that provides a high purity nitrogen\n  1556→environment at low flow levels. The system uses a\n  1557→proprietary gas flow distribution method through the\n  1558→chamber walls to uniformly blanket the chamber with the\n  1559→process nitrogen. A slight positive pressure inside the\n  1560→chamber helps minimize external room effects and helps\n  1561→keep oxygen out of the chamber. Entry and exit purge\n  1562→sections are present to provide a nitrogen curtain at the\n  1563→chamber entrance and exit, which also assists in keeping\n  1564→oxygen out of the chamber. Flow meter controlled\n  1565→venturi exhausts are used to balance the flow in the\n  1566→system and to remove process volatiles.\n  1567→\n  1568→The gas system is non-recirculatory, in that the gas is\n  1569→used once and then expended. This has the advantage\n  1570→that process contaminants are not recirculated which\n  1571→helps maintain high nitrogen purity levels. The heat\n  1572→chamber is accessible by unfastening six clamps and\n  1573→elevating the top heater vertically through the use of a\n  1574→crank mechanism. Purity levels of 20 PPM (over incoming purity level) are attainable in all heating zones.\n  1575→Flow rates are in the 1200 to 1600 cubic feet per hour\n  1576→(34 to 45 cubic meters per hour) range. Machines in the\n  1577→field are experiencing total purity levels of 10-15 PPM\n  1578→(including incoming) at flow rates of 1300cubic feet per\n  1579→hour (37 cubic meters per hour). These purity levels are\n  1580→measured with the machine fully loaded with product.\n  1581→\n  1582→~~~\n  1583→\n  1584→~\n  1585→\n  1586→The amount of flow in the oven is more\n  1587→related to the quantity required to accomplish a high purity nitrogen environment,\n  1588→which is considerably less than in the case\n  1589→of a convection oven.\n  1590→\n  1591→PREHEAT TOP (IR1)\n  1592→\n  1593→DRY TOP (IR3)\n  1594→\n  1595→PREFLOW TOP (IR4)\n  1596→\n  1597→REFLOW\n  1598→TOP (IR5)\n  1599→\n  1600→PREHEAT BOTTOM (IR2)\n  1601→\n  1602→DRY BOTTOM (IR3)\n  1603→\n  1604→PREFLOW BOTTOM (IR4)\n  1605→\n  1606→REFLOW\n  1607→BOTTOM (1R6)\n  1608→\n  1609→NOTE: VENTURI EXHAUSTS ARE ACTIVATED BY COMPRESSED AIR,\n  1610→ALL OTHERS MAY BE NITROGEN OR COMPRESSED AIR.\n  1611→\n  1612→18\n  1613→\n  1614→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology\n  1615→\n  1616→\fEDGE CONVEYOR\n  1617→Proper design of the edge conveying system is critical,\n  1618→as typically only 0.17 inches (4 mm) is in contact with\n  1619→the bottom side edges of the product. If the conveyor\n  1620→has lateral warpage of greater than this amount, the\n  1621→product will fall into the oven. Thus, edge conveyor\n  1622→straightness is a key issue in SMT processing.\n  1623→As the conveyor rails heat during the machine warm-up\n  1624→period, they will grow and tend to warp. The patented\n  1625→system from Research, Inc. shown below tensions the\n  1626→rails, which pulls the rail assembly straight. The lever\n  1627→(outlined in dark) is attached to the rail. The bottom side\n  1628→of the lever is attached to an adjustable spring mechanism\n  1629→that runs from end to end in the machine. Approximately\n  1630→1500 lbs (681 kg) of tensioning force is applied to each\n  1631→\n  1632→rail to maintain straightness. The machine frame is made\n  1633→of welded structural tubing in order to maintain its\n  1634→structural integrity.\n  1635→The use of the tensioning system allows the mass of the\n  1636→rails to be maintained at a reasonably low value, which\n  1637→minimizes the thermal affect of the rail on the product.\n  1638→\n  1639→As the conveyo’r rails heat during the machine warm-up period, they will grow and\n  1640→tend to warp. The patented system from\n  1641→Research, Inc. shown below tensions the\n  1642→rails, which pulls the rail assembly straight.\n  1643→\n  1644→-\n  1645→\n  1646→TENSION STRAIGHTENS RAIL\n  1647→\n  1648→SPRING MAINTAINS TENSION\n  1649→\n  1650→REFERENCES\n  1651→1. “The Transformation of American Industry”, Productivity-Quality Systems, Inc. Module #1, page 3, 1986.\n  1652→2. “Radiation Plus Convection: The Total Solution”, Norman R. Cox, Research Inc., U.S.A., NEPCON West 1990.\n  1653→\n  1654→3. “Optimization of Solder Reflow with IR”, Norman R. Cox, Research, Inc., U.S.A., ISHM 1985.\n  1655→4. “Inert 1R Reflow: The Significance of Oxygen Concentration in the Atmosphere”, Colin Lea, National Physics\n  1656→Laboratory, Teddington, U.K., Surface Mount International, 199I.\n  1657→\n  1658→5. Managerial and Engineering Economy, George A. Taylor, D. Van Nostrand Company, 0 1975 by Litton\n  1659→Educational Publishing, Inc., page 3 I .\n  1660→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology\n  1661→\n  1662→19\n  1663→\n  1664→\fPRODUCT MATRIX\n  1665→\n  1666→I\n  1667→\n  1668→4440\n  1669→\n  1670→4470MP 4470XP\n  1671→\n  1672→4470HP 4470XN\n  1673→\n  1674→4470HN 4480XG\n  1675→\n  1676→36\n  1677→N/A\n  1678→\n  1679→36\n  1680→N/A\n  1681→\n  1682→96\n  1683→120\n  1684→\n  1685→114\n  1686→138\n  1687→\n  1688→96\n  1689→N/A\n  1690→\n  1691→148\n  1692→NIA\n  1693→\n  1694→96\n  1695→N/A\n  1696→\n  1697→914\n  1698→\n  1699→914\n  1700→\n  1701→2438\n  1702→3048\n  1703→\n  1704→2895\n  1705→3505\n  1706→\n  1707→2438\n  1708→\n  1709→3759\n  1710→\n  1711→2438\n  1712→\n  1713→9-1 2\n  1714→\n  1715→9-1 2\n  1716→\n  1717→24-32\n  1718→30-40\n  1719→\n  1720→30-40\n  1721→36-48\n  1722→\n  1723→24-32\n  1724→\n  1725→36-50\n  1726→\n  1727→24-32\n  1728→\n  1729→20-30 20-30\n  1730→\n  1731→60-80\n  1732→76-1 02\n  1733→\n  1734→70-100\n  1735→90-1 20\n  1736→\n  1737→60-80\n  1738→\n  1739→90-130\n  1740→\n  1741→60-80\n  1742→\n  1743→X\n  1744→\n  1745→X\n  1746→\n  1747→X\n  1748→\n  1749→X\n  1750→\n  1751→X\n  1752→\n  1753→X\n  1754→\n  1755→X\n  1756→\n  1757→X\n  1758→\n  1759→X\n  1760→\n  1761→X\n  1762→\n  1763→X\n  1764→X\n  1765→\n  1766→CONVEYOR TYPE\n  1767→EDGE\n  1768→MESH\n  1769→DUAL (EDGE/MESH)\n  1770→\n  1771→N/A\n  1772→STD\n  1773→N/A\n  1774→\n  1775→STD\n  1776→N/A\n  1777→N/A\n  1778→\n  1779→STD\n  1780→OPT\n  1781→OPT\n  1782→\n  1783→STD\n  1